This is a contract test for ArrayList. The contract test checks pre and post conditions of the ArrayList class for different methods and also checks invariants between different calls
The main focus is not on ArrayList but on the postconditions and invariants. So I tried to focus on the contract tests for the methods which are supposed to work on ArrayList class. 

Since Pex does not support constraints with complex expressions such as [PexAssumeUnderTest("arrList.Count >= 1 && arrList.Contains(x)")] it would be very difficult to express the preconditions correctly.

AddContract, RemoveContract, InsertContract, SetContract, GetContract, ContainsContract, IndexOfContract, LastIndexOfContract

I have written the contracts for each of these methods which cover the following aspects:
1. Precondition - What state the method expects from the caller. 
   a. AddContract - Expects the arrList.Count before the method is called and expects this count to be one plus the count before the method is called.
   b. RemoveContract - Expect that arrList.Contains(x) is true before the method is called.
   c. InsertContract - Expects that index is less than arrList.Count and greater than or equals to 0.
   d. SetContract - Expect that index is less than arrList.Count and greater than or equals to 0.
   e. GetContract - Expect that arrList is not empty and index is less than arrList.Count and greater than or equals to 0.
   f. ContainsContract - Expects true since x is in the arrList.
   g. IndexOfContract - Expects that x is in the arrList and it returns the position of x.
   h. LastIndexOfContract - Expects that x is in the arrList and it returns the position of the last occurrence of x.

2. Postconditions - What state the method guarantees to its callers.
   a. AddContract - Count of arrList is one more than before the method was called plus the arrList.Contains(x) is true after the method has been called.
   b. RemoveContract - arrList.Contains(x) is false after the method has been called.
   c. InsertContract - IndexOf(x) is the value of index.
   d. SetContract - IndexOf(x) is the value of index.
   e. GetContract - The element at index position has returned the correct value.
   f. ContainsContract - arrList still contains x.
   g. IndexOfContract - arrList.IndexOf(x) is the greatest index of occurence of x in arrList.
   h. LastIndexOfContract - arrList.LastIndexOf(x) is the greatest index of occurence of x in arrList.

3. Contracts across method calls
   POSTCONDITIONS OF PREVIOUS METHOD IS OBTAINED FROM NEXT METHOD CALL

Please note that while writing the contracts, I kept the assumption (IsTrue) for initial conditions. But I would recommend adding real constraints where possible. The Pex tool does not support constraints with complex expressions for pre-conditions. The only way to express these pre-conditions is by assumptions.

The problem is that I have to execute the tests with the Visual Studio Test (Test Explorer). But when I am executing Pex on the API and testing the contract of these methods, it is just crashing. I am not sure why it is crashing. May be there is some fundamental misunderstanding regarding how to write these tests.

It would be also helpful if you let me know how to verify the test results for the contract tests. Is there a standard way to do that? Do we need to verify the preconditions, postconditions, or both as well as any invariants?

Looking forward to your insights.


A: It's hard to say whether the crash is a bug in Pex. Try using the /export:<file> Pex command-line option to export the failing test to a file and then run the test in the debugger. Check the stack trace to see if you can make any sense of it.--------------------            int Ret_val = New_Ret;
            PexAssume.OutRange(Ret_val < 0, (Old_x < 0 || Old_arrListIndexOfX <= Ret_val));

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$Ret_val", Ret_val);

        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_ClearContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            // Assumption
            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_Capacity = arrList.Capacity;
            int[] Old_ToArray = arrList.ToArray();

            // Operation under test
            arrList.Clear();

            // Postconditions
            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_Capacity = arrList.Capacity;
            int[] New_ToArray = arrList.ToArray();

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Capacity", Old_Capacity);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Capacity", New_Capacity);

            CollectionAssert.AreEqual(New_ToArray, new int[0]);
        }
    }
}

